\section{What it tests}{

RELAX fits omega classes on reference branches and the same classes raised to a power
\code{k} on test branches. \code{k < 1} moves every class towards 1: \strong{relaxation} (weaker
purifying and weaker positive selection). \code{k > 1} moves them away from 1:
\strong{intensification}. The test compares \code{k = 1} with a free \code{k}. It is not a test of
positive selection: intensification can mean stronger purifying selection, and a
gene under positive selection can show either. Read \code{k} with the omega classes.
}

\section{Branch sets}{

The foreground file's rows are the test set. Rows with \code{set} = \code{reference} give the
reference set; without them, every branch outside the test set is the reference. Both
sets must remain identifiable after pruning, and may not share a branch; otherwise the
orthogroup is excluded with a reason (\verb{reference_*}, \code{test_reference_overlap}). Branches
in neither set are unclassified, with their own parameters.
}

\section{Outputs}{

In \code{file.path(output_dir, run_name)}: \verb{_results.tsv} (one row per orthogroup: \code{k},
\code{direction}, \code{lrt}, \code{p_value}, \code{p_adj}, likelihoods, and the omega classes of each set),
\verb{_branches.tsv} (with each branch's \code{set}), \verb{_sequences.tsv}, \verb{_provenance.json}, and
\verb{hyphy/<orthogroup>/}. \code{p_adj} is Benjamini-Hochberg across tested orthogroups.
}

